@@s001 Examples: Nil, None, null in Scala
@@s002 <span>Examples · lists and Option</span>
@@s003 Examples: Nil, None, null in Scala
@@s004 The code of the <a href="../teoria/s03-vuoti.html">theory page</a>: a file that compiles and runs. Checked with Scala 3.8.4 and the course options (<code>-Werror</code>, <code>-source:future</code>).
@@s005 The whole file
@@s006 Run it with <code>scala-cli run NilNone.scala</code>.
@@s007 NilNone.scala
@@s008 Directives for <code>scala-cli</code>: Scala version and checks. <code>-Werror</code> turns warnings into errors.
@@s009 List built with <code>::</code> (adds at the front) and closed by <code>Nil</code>, the empty list. The printed result is <code>List(1, 2)</code>.
@@s010 List with no elements: <code>Nil</code> is a valid list, and <code>isEmpty</code> says <code>true</code>.
@@s011 Two <code>Option</code>s: <code>Some(3)</code> has a value, <code>None</code> says there is nothing.
@@s012 Function that may have no result. The pattern <code>Nil</code> recognises the empty list and returns <code>None</code>, instead of an invented value.
@@s013 <code>Nil</code> is a list: <code>isEmpty</code> and <code>==</code> work as for any list.
@@s014 With a non-empty list it gives <code>Some(1)</code>, with <code>Nil</code> it gives <code>None</code>: the caller must handle both cases.
@@s015 <code>getOrElse</code> gives the value inside <code>Some</code>, or the fallback if it is <code>None</code>.
@@s016 <code>Option(x)</code> converts <code>null</code> into <code>None</code>: the safe way to bring a Java value into Scala.
@@s017 A non-null value becomes <code>Some(ciao)</code>.
@@s018 They are different values: the comparison gives <code>false</code>. Do not confuse the two ways of saying nothing.
@@s019 The compiler accepts <code>null</code> as a <code>String</code>. The risk is using it: the comparison with <code>null</code> gives <code>true</code>.
@@s020 Asking a <code>null</code> for <code>.length</code> throws <code>NullPointerException</code>: that is why one converts to <code>Option</code> straight away.
@@s021 The output
@@s022 Output of the run
@@s023 The list built with <code>::</code> and closed by <code>Nil</code>.
@@s024 <code>Nil</code> is empty and equal to <code>List()</code>.
@@s025 The function with <code>Nil</code> as input gives <code>None</code>.
@@s026 <code>getOrElse</code> chooses the value or the fallback: <code>3</code> and <code>0</code>.
@@s027 <code>Option(null)</code> gives <code>None</code>, <code>Option("ciao")</code> gives <code>Some(ciao)</code>.
@@s028 The comparison between <code>None</code> and <code>null</code> gives <code>false</code>.
@@s029 <code>testo == null</code> gives <code>true</code>; the <code>NullPointerException</code> is caught by the <code>try</code>.
